# Catching Exceptions in JavaScript

Use a `try...catch` statement:

```javascript
try {
  // Code that might throw an error
  const result = JSON.parse(invalidJson);
} catch (error) {
  // This runs if an error occurs
  console.log("Something went wrong:", error.message);
}
```

## The `finally` Block

Add `finally` for code that should run regardless of whether an error occurred:

```javascript
try {
  doSomethingRisky();
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error);
} finally {
  // Always runs — useful for cleanup
  cleanup();
}
```

## The Error Object

The caught error typically has useful properties:

```javascript
try {
  throw new Error("Something broke!");
} catch (error) {
  console.log(error.name);    // "Error"
  console.log(error.message); // "Something broke!"
  console.log(error.stack);   // Stack trace
}
```

## Catching Specific Error Types

Use `instanceof` to handle different errors differently:

```javascript
try {
  someOperation();
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof TypeError) {
    console.log("Type error:", error.message);
  } else if (error instanceof RangeError) {
    console.log("Range error:", error.message);
  } else {
    throw error; // Re-throw unexpected errors
  }
}
```

## Async Code

For promises, use `.catch()` or `try...catch` with `async/await`:

```javascript
// With async/await
async function fetchData() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    return await response.json();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Fetch failed:", error);
  }
}

// With promise chains
fetch(url)
  .then(response => response.json())
  .catch(error => console.error("Fetch failed:", error));
```

**Note:** `try...catch` only catches *synchronous* errors unless you're awaiting the async operation. A plain `try...catch` around `setTimeout` or an un-awaited promise won't catch errors from inside them.
